                <pb facs="0292" n="174" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg174"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.00 Presidential Campaigns/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the High Commissioner in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Russell</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-12-02T19:00:00-05:00">December 2, 1929—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>58. Your 87, November 29, 10 a.m. You may inform President Borno that this
                    Government has been much gratified to learn of his definite statement to the
                    Council of State that he will not be a candidate in the approaching elections,
                    because the statement will refute the charges which have persistently been made
                    by unfriendly elements that the President intended, in spite of the provisions
                    of the Haitian Constitution, to bring about his own reelection. The President’s
                    action again shows his patriotic devotion to the best interests <pb facs="0293"
                        n="175" xml:id="pg175"/> of Haiti and the lofty ideals which have inspired
                    his conduct as the head of the Haitian state.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
